This is a simple Python implementation of the Genetic Algorithm demonstration at www.ai-junkie.com
The original code was in C++. I felt the need of coding the thing myself, and decided to do it in Python.

The problem we solve is:
    "For any given 'target' number, find an expression invlolving any legal combination of 
    addition(+), subtraction(-), multiplication(*) and division(/) on digits that represents the 
    given target number.

    e.g., for the target 15, "9 + 3 * 2" is a solution."

    **Important: In Fup's code, the evaluation of the expressions generated was simple, left to right
    evaluation. I have used normal arithmetic precedence based evaluation(see `expr.py')

Data ingestion
--------------
`data_sources/` and `ingest.py` pull historical OHLCV bars into one canonical
schema (UTC-indexed timestamp + open/high/low/close/volume) so the same
instrument can be compared across providers. The first source is
`histdata.com`'s free ASCII bar data (`data_sources/histdata.py`).

    pip install -r requirements.txt
    python3 ingest.py --instrument EURUSD --timeframe H1 --start 2023-01-01 --end 2023-12-31

This writes a CSV to `data/raw/histdata/EURUSD/H1.csv`. Note that
histdata.com's download flow isn't a plain link - the download page embeds a
one-time form token that must be POSTed to get.php to receive the ZIP; see
`data_sources/histdata.py` for that flow. Run `python3 -m pytest tests/` to
exercise the token/CSV parsing against fixtures without hitting the network.

To add another source for comparison, implement `DataSource.fetch()`
(`data_sources/base.py`) and register it in `data_sources.SOURCES`.

